WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.420
Now that it's here, it will never let you
go.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.940
On October 28, Sony Pictures unveiled the
first trailer for the upcoming Grudge reboot

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:14.070
an R-rated refresh of the 2004 supernatural
horror film of the same name, which itself

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.300
was an American remake of the 2002 Japanese
horror classic Ju-On: The Grudge.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.320
Like the 2004 version of The Grudge and the
film it was inspired by, director Nicolas

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.460
Pesce's reboot of the property roots itself
in the same blood-chilling curse: When someone

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.910
dies "in the grip of rage," an entity forms
killing everyone who comes in contact with

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.960
it and passing on the Grudge in an endless
cycle.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.380
That said, it should come as no surprise that
the first trailer for the reboot of The Grudge

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.480
features several references to the films that
came before it.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.960
These are the most important details in the
Grudge reboot trailer.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.960
Early on in the trailer for the Grudge reboot,
we watch real estate agent Peter Spencer enter

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.730
the house he and his wife are selling, head
upstairs, and lean down to look at the bathtub

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.540
mysteriously filled with jet-black water.

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:06.020
As he brings his face closer to the tub, a
pair of hands break the surface of the water

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.380
and grab the sides of Peter's head.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.840
What the heck just happened there?

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:15.550
Obviously, the entity or entities living within
the house aren't afraid to make their existence

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.120
known, and will lash out in any way they can.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.180
It's also clear that one of the ghosts is
taking hold in the same location where a supernatural

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.920
beast present in The Grudge liked to hide
out: the bathroom.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.420
Specifically, it was Toshio the dead son of
Takeo Saeki, who killed his wife Kayako after

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.500
discovering she was in love with another man.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.840
In the American version of the films, Takeo
drowned young Toshio in the bathtub.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:40.840
"(speaking Japanese)"

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.680
The Grudge reboot director Nicolas Pesce previously
explained to IGN that the drowning motif,

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.720
quote, "keeps coming back" throughout the
new film, given that, quote, "the nature of

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.049
the bathtub is that part of the initial crime
that started this all was a drowning."

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:05.500
But is the evil spirit shown in the bathtub
within the first Grudge reboot trailer actually

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.479
Toshio, or is it another entity entirely?

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:12.340
Pesce noted this time to Collider that the
Grudge reboot takes place at a "slightly different"

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.849
time than the other flicks, so there's a chance
that the body in the bathtub is indeed Toshio,

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:20.800
who may have resurfaced in a new location
to continue haunting the innocent.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.020
Pair that information with the fact that the
trailer also includes a shot of what appears

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.680
to be a ghostly being peeking over the edge
of the bathtub, and it's looking like Toshio

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.020
will make a grisly comeback in the new Grudge
movie.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.440
On the subject of horrifying spirits from
the Grudge franchise, it looks as though Toshio's

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.220
slain mother, Kayako, also appears in the
trailer for the flick.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:46.920
There's a moment that shows a character named
Fiona walking in broad daylight, unaware that

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.160
a ghost is behind her.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.880
With her white skin, black hair draped over
her face, and dropped-open mouth, the spirit

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.700
bears a striking resemblance to Kayako whose
presence is also felt later in the trailer,

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.000
when we see a body tumbling down the center
of a stairwell.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:06.830
Both fans of Ju-On: The Grudge and The Grudge
alike as well as people who have never seen

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.840
the films before will know that Kayako has
a strong connection to stairs: she famously

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.950
crawled down them in the most nightmarish
of manners as she was closing in on one of

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:16.600
her victims.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.140
Maybe she's the one who threw the body down
the stairwell, or perhaps her appearance caused

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:22.800
the person to leap to their death.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.260
Pesce has confirmed that the curse happening
in the U.S. is, quote, "connected to the one

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:32.430
in Japan," and teased that it had "been brought"
to the country like a malignant disease.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.420
Pesce told IGN:

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.420
"The Grudge is kind of like a virus it spreads
and is contagious.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.709
So much of what we wanted to do with this
movie is show that, yes, you know very well

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:45.530
what's been happening in Japan, but what you
don't realize is this is happening in other

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:49.110
places too, and it's not just contained to
this one house in Japan.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.600
It's here and it's here and it's here, and
it's everywhere."

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:57.580
At several points in the footage, including
the pre-roll stinger, we see a gray-haired

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.340
woman on the brink of insanity.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:04.180
She's first shown laughing maniacally in a
kitchen with blood running down her chin and

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:04.680
neck.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:09.760
She then pops up looking less human than before
with her hands blackened and curled up, her

00:04:09.769 --> 00:04:13.270
eyes sunken in, and her mouth dropped open
in a pained yell.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:17.620
This character may be a new one in the Grudge
universe, but the actress who plays her has

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.820
been at the horror game for decades now.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:25.060
She's Lin Shaye, a bonafide scream queen who
has appeared in films like A Nightmare on

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:30.430
Elm Street, Critters, the Ouija movies and,
perhaps most famously, Insidious.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:35.930
This time around, Shaye plays the creepy cackling
woman named Faith Matheson who's clearly affected

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:40.389
by the Grudge in some way, and may even be
revealed as the original owner of the house

00:04:40.389 --> 00:04:42.230
where the curse has taken hold.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:44.720
Did she bring the Grudge to the States from
Japan?

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.040
Is she the woman who committed the killing
in the Grudged home?

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.860
We suspect Faith will be a major player in
the upcoming movie.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:57.009
If this first trailer didn't scare you away,
catch The Grudge in theaters on January 3,

00:04:57.009 --> 00:04:57.580
2020.

